Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 7   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["General Curves and Polydifferentiation",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["28 September 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subtitle",
  FontSize->18],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Polynomials and Curves", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Homogeneous polynomials", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some algebraic definitions and key facts", "Subsubsection",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We will work with ",
  StyleBox["polynomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in our two (dual) sets of variables, namely, ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["uvw.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "By a ",
  StyleBox["monomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " we mean an expression of the form",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^i\ y\^j\ z\^k\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]],
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["(or ",
    CellFrame->True],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[
          SuperscriptBox[
            StyleBox["u",
              FontSlant->"Plain"], "i"],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold",
          FontSlant->"Plain"], 
        StyleBox[
          SuperscriptBox[
            StyleBox["v",
              FontSlant->"Plain"], "j"],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold",
          FontSlant->"Plain"], 
        StyleBox[
          SuperscriptBox[
            StyleBox["w",
              FontSlant->"Plain"], "k"],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  StyleBox["),",
    CellFrame->True],
  " where the ",
  StyleBox["degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the monomial is calculated by the sum  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(i + j + k\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  CellFrame->False,
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here are lists of the ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-monomials up to degree ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TableForm[{Bxyz/@Range[0, 5]}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Let us look at the cardinalitie of these sets:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[Length, %, {2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The number of monomials (in three variables) of degree ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is \t\t    \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(1\/2\ \((2 + 3\ n + n\^2)\)\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          CellFrame->True,
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(1\/2\ \((n + 1)\)\ \((n + 2)\)\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Proof.",
  StyleBox["  Think of a monomial the form",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^i\ y\^j\ z\^k\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]],
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["where ",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(i + j + k == n\)]],
  StyleBox[".  The possible choices of ",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["i", "InlineInput",
    CellFrame->True],
  StyleBox[" are integers in ",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["Range[0, n]", "InlineInput",
    CellFrame->True],
  StyleBox[".  Once ",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["i", "InlineInput",
    CellFrame->True],
  StyleBox[" is chosen, then ",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["j", "InlineInput",
    CellFrame->True],
  StyleBox[" will be chosen from ",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["Range[0, n-i]", "InlineInput",
    CellFrame->True],
  StyleBox[".  After that, the value of ",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["k", "InlineInput",
    CellFrame->True],
  StyleBox[
  " is fixed.  Therefore, the total number of choices is given by the formula \
",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" can work out symbolically this way:",
    CellFrame->True,
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ss = Together[\[Sum]\+\(i = 0\)\%n\((n + 1 - i)\)]\), 
    \(Factor[ss]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Q.E.D.", "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell["\<\
Here is a test that the formula is giving us the right \
numbers:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Function[n, 1\/2\ \((2 + 3\ n + n\^2)\)]/@Range[0, 6]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "From our experience in algebra, we know that a  ",
  StyleBox["polynomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", of course, is just ",
  StyleBox["a linear combination",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of monomials with scalars as coefficients.  And scalar multiples, sums \
and products of polynomials are again regarded as polynomials. We should \
imagine the polynomials as expanded out as a sum of multiples of monomials, \
and the ",
  StyleBox["degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a (mixed) polynomial is the ",
  StyleBox["highest degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of any monomial in its (unique) expansion.  We will work with a special \
class of polynomials."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A polynomial is  ",
  StyleBox["homogeneous",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of its monomials are of the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Every polynomial is uniquely the sum of non-zero homogeneous polynomials \
of different degrees."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Expand out the polynomial and then group the monomials of like degree \
together. ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The decomposition of a polynomial into homogeneous parts can be computed \
in the following way.  Make a substitution of the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({x \[Rule] t\ x, y \[Rule] t\ y, z \[Rule] t\ z}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and then group together the coefficient list of powers of ",
  StyleBox["t", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (which is a primitive ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  operation).   We recall that ",
  StyleBox["CoefficientList[poly, var]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " gives a list of coefficients of powers of ",
  StyleBox["var", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in ",
  StyleBox["poly", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", starting with power ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Here is an example on a random polynomial: "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(poly = 
      x\^2\ \((y - z)\)\^6 + \((x\^2 + y + 2)\)\^7 + \((1 + z)\)\^14\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TableForm[
      CoefficientList[
        poly /. {x \[Rule] t\ x, y \[Rule] t\ y, z \[Rule] t\ z}, t]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[poly] == Plus@@%\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
This result suggests the following test for being homogeneous:\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[x, y, z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is homogeneous of degree ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["if, and only if,",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " it satisfies the equation:  \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[k\ x, k\ y, k\ z] == k\^n\ f[x, y, z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", \nfor all scalars ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["k",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The full proof requires some facts from commutative algebra reviewed in \
the next section. The result implies that if one representation of a \
projective point in homogeneous coordinates satisfies the equation  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[x, y, z] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  then so does ",
  StyleBox["every other",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " representation.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In other words, ",
  StyleBox["a homogeneous xyz-polynomial has geometric significance ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "as defining a ",
  StyleBox["locus of points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 0.4, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A ",
  StyleBox["locus",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  is a place where something happens.  Among geometric objects we find \
various types, such as points, lines, curves, transformations, and so on.  \
When certain conditions are given, we speak of the ",
  StyleBox["locus of all objects",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(of a given type)",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["satisfying the conditions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[". ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " There is really no difference between the words ",
  StyleBox["locus ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "and ",
  StyleBox["set",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", except that locus has a more geometric flavor."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some basic theorems from algebra", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Some of the following facts are easy to prove and some are hard.  Whether \
time should be taken in an undergraduate course to prove them depends on the \
instructor, on the time available, and on the preparation of the students.  \
For the time being, we are content to ",
  StyleBox["list",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the results.  The first theorem we discussed in some detail during the \
first two weeks when we talked about ",
  StyleBox["The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Over the ",
  StyleBox["complex numbers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", every polynomial in ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " variable of degree ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can be factored into ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["linear factors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Hence, a polynomial equation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[z] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has exactly ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " roots, provided that ",
  StyleBox["multiplicities",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are counted."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["The following theorem requires more algebraic insight.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Every ",
  StyleBox["multivariate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial can be factored into ",
  StyleBox["irreducible factors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that cannot be further factored.  The factorization is ",
  StyleBox["unique",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " up to scalar multiples."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The theorem includes the principle that if an irreducible polynomial \
divides a product of polynomials, then it must divide (",
  StyleBox["i.e.,",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " be a factor of) one or the other of the polynomials in the product."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The theorem holds over many rings, not just over the complex numbers.  One \
should ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " conclude that multivariate polynomials can be factored into ",
  StyleBox["linear factors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", however, since that is far from the truth.  (An example is the \
polynomial  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", as we shall be able to prove later.)  Since we will be concerned mainly \
with homogeneous polynomials, we need the following."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If a ",
  StyleBox["homogeneous",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial factors, then the factors are themselves ",
  StyleBox["homogeneous",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["\<\
The following theorems are key to showing that the algebra we do \
with polynomials fits the geometry.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A (homogeneous) polynomial of several variables that vanishes at all ",
  StyleBox["rational",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is also sufficient to consider only ",
  StyleBox["integer",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points.  The theorem is often used to say that if a polynomial does not \
reduce formally to ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (by algebraic laws), then there is a ",
  StyleBox["substitution",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of numerical values for the variables so that the value of the polynomial \
is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Finally, we note that zeros identify factors.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "is an ",
  StyleBox["irreducible",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial, and if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[x, y, z] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " always implies ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g[x, y, z] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the polynomial ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Curves, envelopes, and intersections", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  PageBreakAbove->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Curves and envelopes", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In the algebraic approach, an object in (projective) geometry is usually \
given by its (homogeneous) coordinates.  Because homogeneous coordinates \
multiplied through by a common scalar represent the same object, conditions \
in projective geometry are often given as a set of ",
  StyleBox["homogeneous polynomial equations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The locus defined by a set of equations is generallly called a ",
  StyleBox["variety",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In the projective plane, the locus of zeros of a homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "polynomial of degree ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is called a ",
  StyleBox["curve of degree n",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A curve is ",
  StyleBox["degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "(or ",
  StyleBox["reducible",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") if the polynomial defining it can be factored into factors of lower \
degree.  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "One of the theorems of commutative algebra mentioned earlier assures us \
that a non-degenerate curve (as a locus of points) determines its polynomial \
uniquely up to a scalar multiple.  (Why?)  Note that a degenerate curve (as a \
set) can always be represented of as the ",
  StyleBox["union",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of non-degenerate curves.  (Why?)  Note, too, that a factor can occur \
several times, and a ",
  StyleBox["power",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a polynomial determines the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " locus of zeros as the original polynomial."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We have not spoken of dual polynomials very much, since the ",
  StyleBox["algebraic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " facts are the same.  But, geometirically, duality is very interesting."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox["In the projective plane, the locus of zeros of a homogeneous ",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox["uvw-",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox["polynomial of degree ",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox[" is called a ",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox["curve of degree n",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontSlant->"Italic",
    Background->None],
  StyleBox["  ",
    Background->None]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Since a dual curve is a locus of ",
  StyleBox["lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we can visualize this as the ",
  StyleBox["envelope",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a curve being swept out by the lines.  An interesting ",
  StyleBox["problem",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is whether the set of ",
  StyleBox["tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  to a curve form a dual curve as a locus.  To solve this question, we \
have to wait until we can define precisely what it means to be a tangent \
line.  In general, there is a somewhat difficult algebraic problem of \
relating curves and envelopes."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 0.4, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Linear equations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " have lines (or, dually, pencils) as their loci.  Lines ",
  StyleBox["are",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " curves, but they are not very curved!  The first interesting case is that \
of the ",
  StyleBox["conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Historically, a ",
  StyleBox["conic section",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  is defined as the intersection of a plane with a cone.  If we developed \
this definition and used higher-dimensional spaces, we would find that \
certain cones (as surfaces) were characterized by rather simple quadratic \
equations.  In any plane we could set up ",
  StyleBox["local coordinates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The locus of the intersection would be found by substituting these \
coordinates into the equation for the cone.  Thus, the equation for a conic \
section is always a ",
  StyleBox["quadratic equation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For expediency we use this",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["algebraic definition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with quadratic polynomials as the definition of  a conic section, but it \
ought to be justified by proving that ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  quadratic equation can be obtained by taking a section of a cone.  We \
will have to be satisfied by showing later that all (non-degenerate) conics \
are projective images of ",
  StyleBox["circles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We will treat the space of quadratic homogeneous polynomials as a ",
  StyleBox["vector space",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " giving us a ",
  StyleBox["projective space of conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In alphabetical order there are nine quadratic monomials in the set"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{"                ", 
        StyleBox[\({x\^2, x\ y, x\ z, y\ x, y\^2, y\ z, z\ x, z\ y, z\^2}\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ","
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "but, after communitivity is applied, there are only ",
  StyleBox["six",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Therefore, the ",
  StyleBox["linear-space dimension",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the space of quadratic homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials is ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", but the ",
  StyleBox["projective dimension",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["5",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  We will count dimensions for higher-degree polynomials later."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A conic is ",
  StyleBox["degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  if it factors into ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  factors.  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
The locus of points on such a conic is thus the union of two lines \
(or, possibly, one line counted twice).  Theorems about conics are developed \
in later lectures, including a test for degeneracy. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Here is a hint about thinking of spaces of curves as geometruc \
spaces in their own right.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In any linear space, a ",
  StyleBox["pencil ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is any two-dimensional subspace.  (A ",
  StyleBox["ray",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  is a one-dimensional subspace.)  In projective terms, a pencil is a \
line",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "in the projective space.  In the space of quadratic polynomials, we speak \
of a",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["pencil of conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Intersecting lines with curves and curves with curves", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
There is indeed a geometric meaning to the degree of a polynomial \
defining a curve.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A line through a point ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on a curve of degree ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["cuts",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (or ",
  StyleBox["intersects",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") the curve in exactly ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " points (counting multiplicities of roots).  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[x, y, z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a homogeneous polynomial of degree ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["P",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a point  not on the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[x, y, z] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (There must be such!  Why?)  The points ",
  StyleBox["distinct from",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["P",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on a line through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["P",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " can be parameterized by (non-homogeneous) linear forms (in a variable ",
  StyleBox["t", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", say) by writing ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t\ P + Q\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", where ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a ",
  StyleBox["second point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the line.  ",
  StyleBox["Substituting",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the coordinates of this point on the line into the equation, we get a \
polynomial of degree ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in the single variable ",
  StyleBox["t", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This polynomial has ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " roots, corresponding exactly to ",
  StyleBox["the intersections of the line with the curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Thus, a line intersects a ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in one or two points.  And, please note, that because we use complex \
numbers, algebraic equations in one variable ",
  StyleBox["always",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  have solutions; so, a line ",
  StyleBox["always",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  intersects a curve.  There is a more general result."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["B\[EAcute]zout's Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are homogeneous polynomials of degrees ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and if the two curves ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " intersect in",
  StyleBox[" more than",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n\ m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "points, then ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " share a non-trivial common factor.  And, if the polynomials ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  ",
  StyleBox["do not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "share a non-trivial common factor, then the two curves intersect in \
exactly ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n\ m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "points (again counting multiplicities suitably).  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The proof is quite hard and requires some rather subtle algebra.  We \
cannot prove it in this course, but we can take it as a guideline for the ",
  StyleBox["reasonableness",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of various results we find by computation."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Polydifferentiation and Duality", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Polydifferentiation", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Defining polydifferentiation", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In order to define and investigate properties of curves defined by \
polynomials, we have to turn to the use of ",
  StyleBox["differentiation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consider the space of all ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials as a space of (continuous) infinitely differentiable \
multivariate functions.  On such functions ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ",",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "the ",
  StyleBox["operators of",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["partial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["differentiation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", namely,\n\t\t\t",
  StyleBox["\[PartialD]/\[PartialD]x", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["\[PartialD]/\[PartialD]y", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["\[PartialD]/\[PartialD]z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ",\nare ",
  StyleBox["associative",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["commutative",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (under composition).  This is proved in calculus books about functions of \
several variables.  And the use of complex numbers makes no difference."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, these  compositions are also ",
  StyleBox["linear operators",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and any ",
  StyleBox["linear combination",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of  linear operators is again a linear operator.  Moreover, composition \
of linear operators distributes over all linear combinations of operators; \
hence, we conclude:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The differential operators generate a ",
  StyleBox["commutative",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["algebra of operators",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  "
}], "Text",
  CellFrame->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is therefore possible to define a ",
  StyleBox["ring homomorphism",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  from the (free) ring of ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials to these operators on the ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials.  Or, more simply, we can just ",
  StyleBox["interpret",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials as ",
  StyleBox["being",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " partial differential operators on ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials.  That is, think of \n\t\t",
  StyleBox["u == \[PartialD]/\[PartialD]x", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ",  ",
  StyleBox["v == \[PartialD]/\[PartialD]y", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["w == \[PartialD]/\[PartialD]z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \nin a ",
  StyleBox["symbolic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " sense; next, think of ",
  StyleBox["multiplication",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as ",
  StyleBox["composition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of operators; and, finally, think of ",
  StyleBox["addition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as ",
  StyleBox["summing",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " linear operators pointwise.  We will see later that this ring \
homomorphism is actually an ",
  StyleBox["isomorphism",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We note that a first-degree differentiation of a homogeneous polynomial is \
again a homogeneous polynomial of one lower degree.  Compositions of \
operators will continue to lower the degree and keep the result homogeneous.  \
Thus, any ",
  StyleBox["homogeneous",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial determines a differential operator that will take a \
homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial of a certain degree and map it to a lower-degree homogeneous \
polynomial, where the degree has been lowered by ",
  StyleBox["exactly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the degree of the operator."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "This way just described of interpreting polynomials of one kind as \
differential operators on polynomilas of another kind is called ",
  StyleBox["polydifferentiation.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If the degree of an operator is greater than the degree of of the \
polynomial, then the value of the differentiation is obviously ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We can therefore forget about those cases.  In doing so, we will take \
the opportunity of making the definition of polydifferentiation ",
  StyleBox["symmetric ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "in a certain sense.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In particular, if ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial, and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial, then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is defined as the result of allowing the polynomial of ",
  StyleBox["lower degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to operate on the polynomial of ",
  StyleBox["higher",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "as a differential operator.  We shall argue later that:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In case the two polynomials have the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " degree,",
  StyleBox[
  " it does not matter which of the polynomials is regarded as the operator.",
    
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  CellFrame->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In case ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are linear polynomial, then ",
  StyleBox["PD[f, g]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " computed by differentiation is the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  as the ordinary dot product ."
}], "Text",
  CellFrame->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["\<\
Why?  Because in the linear case differentiation just cancels \
variables leaving the coefficients standing alone.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[a\_1\ x + a\_2\ y + a\_3\ z, b\_1\ u + b\_2\ v + b\_3\ w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, dot product gave us a duality between points and lines, we thus will \
employ the more general polydifferentiation to give us ",
  StyleBox["duality at",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["higher degrees",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[
"Polydifferential graded algebra for \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]general\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] polynomials"], "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["\<\
Here are some examples of using polydifferentiation.  First some \
simple differentiation giving first derivatives.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, d, e, f, Subscript]; \nf\_1 = x\^2 + y\^2 - a\ z\^2; 
    \nf\_2 = b\ x\ y + c\ y\ z + \((d\  + e)\) z\ x; \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TableForm[\((PD[f\_1\ f\_2, #]&)\)/@Buvw[1]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If we want to see homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials, we have to ",
  StyleBox["\"tidy up\"",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TableForm[Tidy/@\((\((PD[f\_1\ f\_2, #]&)\)/@Buvw[1])\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But we can take ",
  StyleBox["linear combinations ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of derivatives as well."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ans = PD[f\_1\ f\_2, u - 2\ v + 3\ w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ans = Expand[ans] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we can ckeck a ",
  StyleBox["Law of Differentiation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ans == 
      Tidy[PD[f\_1, u - 2\ v + 3\ w]\ f\_2 + PD[f\_2, u - 2\ v + 3\ w]\ f\_1]
        \)], "Input"],

Cell["Now here are the second derivatives.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TableForm[\((PD[f\_1\ f\_2, #]&)\)/@Buvw[2]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TableForm[Tidy/@\(\((PD[f\_1\ f\_2, #]&)\)/@Buvw[2]\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And so on.  But, suppose we ",
  StyleBox["declare",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the symbols ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to have the following properties:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, g, Subscript]; \)\), 
    \(f /: DegP[f] = 3; \ng /: DegP[g] = 3; \)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
This, then is all we know about them, and otherwise they are \
general. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
??f
??g\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If we calculate with them, then they behave as ",
  StyleBox["variables",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "for polynomials of degree ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[a\ f + b\ g, w\ u\^2 + c\ w\ v\ w]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
In fact, our rules will give this differential expression a degree:\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["DegP[%]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, we can go on to form algebraic combinations of ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  and ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f\^2 + f\ g + g\^2, w\ u\^2 + c\ u\ v\ w]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Expand[%]", "Input"],

Cell["DegP[%]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Application.",
  StyleBox[
  "  Symbolic computation with symbolic polynomial variables will be very \
helpful in giving ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["proofs",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " For ",
  StyleBox["subscripted variables",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we unfortunately cannot declare their degrees separately.  Instead, we \
have to associate the declaration with ",
  StyleBox["Subscript", "InlineInput"],
  " so we can clear it later."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 0.4, 1]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, g, Subscript]; \nSubscript /: DegP[f\_1] = 4; \n
    Subscript /: DegP[g\_1] = 4; \n\(?f\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?Subscript\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[Subscript]; \n\(?Subscript\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As more a preview of things to come, we do some further examples   First \
we make a list of several very \"basic\" operators of low degree ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " monomials."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ops = Join[Buvw[0], Buvw[1], Buvw[2], Buvw[3]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Remember, the interpretation of polynomials as differential operator is \
going to be given by ",
  StyleBox["PD", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Here are the results for a ",
  StyleBox["symbolic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
ClearAll[f];
f/: DegP[f] = 3;
TableForm[Join[{{\"Derivative\",\"Operator\"}},
                 {PD[f,#],#}&/@ops]]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["Now here is a specific polynomial.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = x\^5 + y\^2\ z\^3 + x\^2\ y\ z\^2; \)\), 
    \(TableForm[
      Join[{{"\<Derivative\>", "\<Operator\>"}}, \(({PD[f, #1], #1}&)\)/@ops]]
      \)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Characterizing polydifferentiation", "Subsubsection",
  PageBreakAbove->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The following ",
  StyleBox["five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " algebraic properties ",
  StyleBox["uniquely determine",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the operation ",
  StyleBox["PD",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " of polydifferentiation operating on pairs of dual homogeneous \
polynomials."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\t1. Applicability. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Input.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["The expression",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["for polydifferentiation is \tintended to be",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["meaningful",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[",  provided that",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["is a homogeneous",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["-polynomial \tand ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["is a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["-homogeneous polynomial.  \n\t",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["Output.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "When the polynomials are homogeneous, then  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a \t\thomogeneous polynomial of degree exactly ",
  StyleBox["the absolute value of the \t\tdifference of the degrees",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (or the ",
  StyleBox["zero polynomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", which has no \tdefinite degree \[LongDash] but is given degree ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " by convention).  \n\tIf ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are of the ",
  StyleBox["same degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["scalar",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  \n\tIf ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has the",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["larger degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is an ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial (or ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ").  \n\tIf ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has the",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["larger degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial (or ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ")."
}], "Text",
  TextAlignment->Left,
  Background->RGBColor[0.2, 1, 0.6]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\t2. Bilinearity.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The expression ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " for polydifferentiation is ",
  StyleBox["bilinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in \tboth its arguments."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0.2, 1, 0.6]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\t3. Associativity.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The operation ",
  StyleBox["PD", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is ",
  StyleBox["associative",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", in the sense that these four \tequations hold:\n",
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    FontFamily->"Courier"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, 1] == f\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox["\t\t\t\n\t\t",
    FontFamily->"Courier"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[1, g] == g\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" \t\t\t\n",
    FontFamily->"Courier"],
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g\_1\ g\_2] == PD[PD[f, g\_1], g\_2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" \t\t\t\n\t\t",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f\_1\ f\_2, g] == PD[f\_1, PD[f\_2, g]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" \t\t\t\n\t",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["provided that",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  the product polynomials do not dominate in degree."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0.2, 1, 0.6]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\t4. Orthogonality. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " The vector space of ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials is \t",
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  to the vector space of ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials with ",
  StyleBox["PD", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "as \tthe ",
  StyleBox["bilinear dual paring",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The two dual bases are ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({x, y, z}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", \trespectively.  "
}], "Text",
  PageBreakAbove->True,
  Background->RGBColor[0.2, 1, 0.6]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\t5. Derivationality.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  ",
  StyleBox["PD", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["derivative",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["operator",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", which means that for ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  \tpolynomials ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "we have:\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{
                SubscriptBox[
                  StyleBox["f",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], \(f\_2\)}], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["g",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "]"}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["PD",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["[",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["f",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox["g",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
                StyleBox["]",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[\(f\_2\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["PD",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["[",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["f",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox["g",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
                StyleBox["]",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], " ", \(f\_1\)}]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(PD[f, g\_1\ g\_2] == PD[f, g\_1]\ g\_2 + PD[f, g\_2]\ g\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0.2, 1, 0.6]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof of the theorem: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["PD",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is defined by using the ring of partial differential operators, and the \
above are all true (and well known) properties of partial differentiation.  \
The reason that polydifferentiation is uniquely determined by these five \
properties shown is that every polynomial is a sum of monomials, and when ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and",
  StyleBox[" g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are monomials, then the value of ",
  StyleBox["PD[f, g]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is completely determined by the laws of associativity and \
derivationality.  By bilineraity the values on arbitrary polynomials must \
threrfore also uniquely be determined.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here is one check involving the variables:", "Text",
  PageBreakAbove->True],

Cell["Outer[PD,Bxyz[1],Buvw[1]] // MatrixForm", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And this is exactly what it means to have dual bases at degree ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["For powers of polynomials and for ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" polynomials ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["and",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["we have the rules:",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\_1\%n, g] == n\ PD[f\_1, g]\ f\_1\%\(n - 1\)\)]],
  "\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, g\_1\%m] == m\ PD[f, g\_1]\ g\_1\%\(m - 1\)\)]],
  "\n",
  StyleBox["If",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["m \[LessEqual] n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[", we have (by iteration of the above formulae) the rules:",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\^n, g\^m] == 
        \(\(\(n!\)\ \)\/\(\((n - m)\)!\)\) PD[f, g]\^m\ f\^\(n - m\)\)]],
  "\n",
  StyleBox["If ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["n \[LessEqual] m", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[", we have",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{"\t          ", 
        StyleBox[\(PD[f\^n, g\^m]\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  StyleBox[" == ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(m!\)\/\(\((m - n)\)!\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\^n\ f\^\(m - n\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\n",
  StyleBox["If ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(n == m\)]],
  StyleBox[", we have",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{"\t          ", 
        StyleBox[\(PD[f\^n, g\^n]\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  StyleBox[" == ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n!\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\^n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox["\t\t\t\t\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Strictly speaking, the above equations have to be proved by ",
  StyleBox["mathematical induction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the exponents."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Duality and evaluation", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The dual vector spaces of polynomials", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The linear spaces (each of dimension ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\ \((n + 1)\)\ \((n + 2)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ") of ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials and ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials are ",
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to each other under ",
  StyleBox["PD", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " as the ",
  StyleBox["bilinear paring",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof (mostly by example): ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " This is the formal statement that justifies the conclusion that when ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " have the same degree, it does not matter in evaluating ",
  StyleBox["PD[f, g]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " which we take as the ",
  StyleBox["function",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and which we take as the ",
  StyleBox["operator",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   The set-up is thus completely symmetric in the symbols.  This is a ",
  StyleBox["sweeping expansion",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the idea of duality between points and lines, as that concerns just \
the polynomials of degre ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Esentially, in considering polynomials of a given degree as forming a ",
  StyleBox["vector space",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the ",
  StyleBox["bases",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can be chosen as the ",
  StyleBox["monomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (In the higher degree cases, an adjustment of a ",
  StyleBox["scalar factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is required in order to get exactly a ",
  StyleBox["dual basis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".)  Recall that the ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree monomials are of the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^i\ y\^j\ z\^k\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(i + j + k == n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[". ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Here follow examples (we have seen before) of the various bases.  \
First the quadatic case.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["{Bxyz[2],Buvw[2]}//TableForm", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The matrix of ",
  StyleBox["PD", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " combination is diagonal.  Why?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pd = Outer[PD, Bxyz[2], Buvw[2]]; \)\), 
    \(MatrixForm[pd]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We had to get a ",
  StyleBox["diagonal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " matrix because in a polydifferentiation between monomials of the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " degree, say, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
          RowBox[{\(x\^i\ y\^j\ z\^k\), ",", 
            RowBox[{
              SuperscriptBox["u", 
                SuperscriptBox["i", "\[Prime]",
                  MultilineFunction->None]], " ", 
              SuperscriptBox["v", 
                SuperscriptBox["j", "\[Prime]",
                  MultilineFunction->None]], " ", 
              SuperscriptBox["w", 
                SuperscriptBox["k", "\[Prime]",
                  MultilineFunction->None]]}]}], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", if the two triples of exponents are not the same, the answer has to be ",
  
  StyleBox["0.", "InlineInput"],
  "  (Why?)  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["We next throw in some square roots.", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
npd = Sqrt[Inverse[pd]];
npd // MatrixForm\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["The modified bases are:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Bxyz[2] . npd, Buvw[2] . npd} // TableForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We try ",
  StyleBox["PD ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "again."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatrixForm[Outer[PD, Bxyz[2] . npd, Buvw[2] . npd]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now we have ",
  StyleBox["dual bases",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " according to the usual definition from Linear Algebra.  The thrid degree \
case is similar."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pd = Outer[PD, Bxyz[3], Buvw[3]]; \)\), 
    \(MatrixForm[pd]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
npd = Sqrt[Inverse[pd]];
npd //MatrixForm\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Bxyz[3] . npd, Buvw[3] . npd} // TableForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatrixForm[Outer[PD, Bxyz[3] . npd, Buvw[3] . npd]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And it should be clear that the method extends to all degrees.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Evaluation Theorem", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Any polynomial ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " may be considered as defining a ",
  StyleBox["function",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  by evaluating it at particular complex numbers.  Treating polynomials as \
",
  StyleBox["expressions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", this evaluation can be done, of course, by a",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["substitution:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f /. {x \[Rule] a, y \[Rule] b, z \[Rule] c}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We now make a very important step to relate ",
  StyleBox["evaluation to polydifferentiation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Evaluation Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial of degree ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and if ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the linear form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then we find\n",
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(PD[f, g\^n] == \(n!\)\ f /. {x \[Rule] a, y \[Rule] b, z \[Rule] c}
            \),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",\nor, if we used a notation of ",
  StyleBox["(homogeneous) polynomial functions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then we could write:\n",
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(PD[f[x, y, z], \((a\ u + b\ v + c\ w)\)\^n] == 
          \(n!\)\ f[a, b, c]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "In view of ",
  StyleBox["bilinearity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["PD", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", this need only be checked for ",
  StyleBox["monomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  In this case the result is proved by the associative law using ",
  StyleBox["induction on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is, if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f == f\_1\ t\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", where ",
  StyleBox["t", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is one of the variables ",
  StyleBox["x, y, z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(PD[f\_1\ t, g\^n] == PD[f\_1, PD[t, g\^n]] == 
          n\ PD[t, g]\ PD[f\_1, g\^\(n - 1\)]\),
        Background->GrayLevel[1]]]]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[t, g] == t /. {x \[Rule] a, y \[Rule] b, z \[Rule] c}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", as is easily checked by bilinearity and duality.  Then, by the inductive \
hypothesis, the right-hand side equals"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\ \ \ \ \ \ 
    \(n\ t\  /. 
        \ {x \[Rule] a, y \[Rule] b, z \[Rule] c} \(\((n - 1)\)!\)\ 
          f\_1\) /. {x \[Rule] a, y \[Rule] b, z \[Rule] c}\)\)],
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The desired answer now emerges, because substitution ",
  StyleBox["commutes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with multiplication. ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The ",
  StyleBox["dual theorem",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " interchanged holds as well, since the characterization theorem  for ",
  StyleBox["PD ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is completely symmetric.  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 0.4, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Geometric Application.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Suppose that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial of degree ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and suppose that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is the linear form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u\  + \ b\ v\  + \ c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", representing a ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The equation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " represents a ",
  StyleBox["curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and the condition for",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["the point to lie on the curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "can be written as\n\t\t ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, g\^n] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 0.300008, 0.300008]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is a direct generalization of what we did for points and lines, and \
it is the key fact that connects the ",
  StyleBox["algebra",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and the ",
  StyleBox["geometry",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The dual equation\n\t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\^n, g] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \n(where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is linear and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial of degree ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") is the condition for the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to lie on the ",
  StyleBox["envelope",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here is an example of evaluation with a monomial:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[x\^2\ y\^3\ z, \((a\ u + b\ v + c\ w)\)\^6]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is a 4th-degree check of this important property:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(PD[x\^4 + 3\ x\^2\ y\^2 - 8\ z\^3\ y, \((a\ u + b\ v + c\ w)\)\^4]\n
    \), 
    \(Factor[%]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Here is a 3rd-degree check in dual form:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[\((a\ x + b\ y + c\ z)\)\^3, d\ u\ v\ w + e\ u\^2\ v]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The Evaluation Theorem also allows us to make a key deduction.\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial (or ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial) is uniquely determined by its behavior as a partial \
differential operator."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Applying a ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial to a ",
  StyleBox["power",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a linear form just evaluates it at a certain triple (in view of the ",
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the Evaluation Theorem).  But a polynomial is formally determined by \
its behavior as a ",
  StyleBox["polynomial function",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Otherwise said, this proves that the ring of partial differential \
operators on polynomials is ",
  StyleBox["isomorphic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the polynomial ring.  We can also derive many important ",
  StyleBox["geometric",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " consequences from the Evaluation Theorem, as we will see in future work.  \
"
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
